1. WHAT IS PUBLIC DOMAIN

Wyclif's Latin, in the Wyclif Society's editions (Pollard's Dialogus and Loserth's
De ecclesia, both 1886), is long out of copyright, as are the editors'
marginal summaries. No claim is made over them.
A faithful transcription of a public-domain text creates no new copyright,
and none is asserted here.

2. WHAT IS LICENSED

The English translation; the notes, headnotes and other prose; and the
encoding as a compilation (the division into passages, the alignment of
Latin and English, the citation scheme and its addressing, and the recorded
readings and corrections) are licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/

3. MACHINE USE

Use of this material to train, fine-tune, evaluate or ground machine-learning
systems, and to build retrieval indexes over it, is permitted, including for
commercial model development, under the terms above: keep the attribution on
the apparatus and say if you changed the text.

4. WHAT NEEDS ASKING

Resale: print or ebook for sale, paywalled databases, commercial reference
products with the text inside. Permission is given readily and free for
scholarly and ecclesial use: wilson@wrootlabs.com

5. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

Files under reference/ and sources/ were made by others and keep their own
